From: KRdp displayless experiment <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH] Keep a frame in flight from straddling a GFX caps re-advertisement

A client that re-advertises its caps drops all its GFX state and ignores every
message until the new CapsConfirm (MS-RDPEGFX 3.3.5.19). KRdp handles the
re-advertisement on the connection thread, while frames, and with them the
surface resets, go out on the frame submission thread, with nothing ordering
the two. A reset in flight could send CreateSurface before the new CapsConfirm,
where the client drops it, and MapSurfaceToOutput after it. mstsc rejects a map
of a surface it does not have with E_INVALIDARG and drops the connection with
reason 3334, so its own recovery failed. Hold a lock for each whole frame and
take it while forgetting the surface on a re-advertisement, and make the flags
both threads touch atomic. Also replace the comment that took re-advertisement
for a fixed mstsc habit with the rule from the spec.

diff --git a/src/VideoStream.cpp b/src/VideoStream.cpp
index 2853a5e..a73e68f 100644
--- a/src/VideoStream.cpp
+++ b/src/VideoStream.cpp
@@ -171,8 +171,20 @@ public:
     uint16_t nextSurfaceId = 1;
     bool enabled = false;
     bool streamingEnabled = false;
-    bool capsConfirmed = false;
+    // Set only once CapsConfirm is written, so that every surface command the
+    // frame thread sends from then on reaches the client behind it.
+    std::atomic_bool capsConfirmed = false;
     bool channelOpen = false;
+    // Serializes the surface state between the frame submission thread, which
+    // holds it for each whole frame, and the connection thread handling a caps
+    // re-advertisement. A client ignores every GFX message from re-advertising
+    // until the new CapsConfirm (MS-RDPEGFX 3.3.5.19). Without the lock, a
+    // reset in flight could send CreateSurface before that CapsConfirm, where
+    // the client drops it, and MapSurfaceToOutput after it. mstsc rejects a map
+    // of a surface it does not have with E_INVALIDARG and drops the connection.
+    // The connection thread must not hold it across a blocking call to the main
+    // thread, as close() on the main thread joins the frame thread.
+    std::mutex surfaceMutex;
 
     std::jthread frameSubmissionThread;
     std::mutex frameQueueMutex;
@@ -549,20 +561,26 @@ bool VideoStream::onChannelIdAssigned(uint32_t channelId)
 
 uint32_t VideoStream::onCapsAdvertise(const RDPGFX_CAPS_ADVERTISE_PDU *capsAdvertise)
 {
-    // Windows clients (mstsc) send CapsAdvertise twice: once during
-    // initial setup and again after confirming. If we already confirmed
-    // caps, this is a GFX channel reset — clear surface state so
-    // surfaces get re-created on the next frame.
+    // A client may advertise its caps again once a CapsConfirm of version 10.3 or
+    // later went out, to reset the protocol: it drops all its GFX state and ignores
+    // every message until the new CapsConfirm (MS-RDPEGFX 3.2.5.18, 3.3.5.19). mstsc
+    // does so to recover from a GFX command it rejected. Clear the surface state, so
+    // that surfaces get re-created on the first frame after the new CapsConfirm.
     if (d->capsConfirmed) {
         qCDebug(KRDP) << "GFX channel reset (re-advertisement), resetting surface state";
-        d->capsConfirmed = false;
-        d->surface->pendingReset = true;
-        // A client re-advertising its caps has reset its whole GFX state, so its
-        // surfaces and encoding contexts are gone already. Telling it to delete
-        // them anyway is a protocol error to mstsc: it rejects DeleteEncodingContext
-        // with E_INVALIDARG and drops the connection (reason 3334). Forget them
-        // on this side only.
-        destroySurface(false);
+        {
+            // Waits out a frame in flight, so that nothing it sends lands after
+            // the new CapsConfirm.
+            std::lock_guard surfaceLock(d->surfaceMutex);
+            d->capsConfirmed = false;
+            d->surface->pendingReset = true;
+            // A client re-advertising its caps has reset its whole GFX state, so its
+            // surfaces and encoding contexts are gone already. Telling it to delete
+            // them anyway is a protocol error to mstsc: it rejects DeleteEncodingContext
+            // with E_INVALIDARG and drops the connection (reason 3334). Forget them
+            // on this side only.
+            destroySurface(false);
+        }
         {
             std::lock_guard lock(d->pendingFramesMutex);
             d->pendingFrames.clear();
@@ -986,6 +1004,10 @@ bool VideoStream::hasInFlightCapacity() const
 
 void VideoStream::sendFrame(const VideoFrame &dequeuedFrame)
 {
+    // Held for the whole frame, so that a caps re-advertisement waits until
+    // everything this frame sends is out, ahead of the new CapsConfirm.
+    std::lock_guard surfaceLock(d->surfaceMutex);
+
     // RemoteFX sends only damaged tiles, so a frame that is dropped after
     // leaving the queue takes its damage with it, and the client keeps stale
     // pixels there until something happens to repaint the area. The queue
@@ -1020,8 +1042,7 @@ void VideoStream::sendFrame(const VideoFrame &dequeuedFrame)
     // A reset gives the client a new, empty surface, so the next frame has to
     // cover all of it, not only what changed since the last one.
     bool surfaceRecreated = false;
-    if (d->surface->pendingReset) {
-        d->surface->pendingReset = false;
+    if (d->surface->pendingReset.exchange(false)) {
         performReset(frame.size);
         surfaceRecreated = true;
     }
diff --git a/src/VideoStreamSurface.h b/src/VideoStreamSurface.h
index 328c528..f1b8079 100644
--- a/src/VideoStreamSurface.h
+++ b/src/VideoStreamSurface.h
@@ -5,6 +5,7 @@
 
 #pragma once
 
+#include <atomic>
 #include <memory>
 
 #include <DmaBufHandler>
@@ -50,7 +51,8 @@ public:
     Surface surface;
     QSize size;
     QSize requestedSize;
-    bool pendingReset = true;
+    /// Set from the main thread and the connection thread, consumed by the frame thread.
+    std::atomic_bool pendingReset = true;
 
 private:
     VideoStream *const m_stream;
